Model, rig, and animate the **Warden Rifleman**, an upright Cobalt infantry soldier
for the Wardens' Siege squad — as **one skinned character** that fits within a
{{voxel.width}}x{{voxel.height}}x{{voxel.depth}} bounding box, authored by driving
**headless Blender** through its Python API. You build the character mesh, invent an
armature (skeleton) to bind it, weight the skin, add an empty `weapon_socket` bone for
the rifle, and **author its six animations** — all by writing a Blender Python script
(`build.py`) and running it.

This test measures **creativity and craft with a real character pipeline**, not
instruction-following: the brief tells you *what the Rifleman is* and *how it must
move*, but the mesh topology, the skeleton you build, where you place the joints, how
you weight the skin, and how you shape every form are **entirely yours to invent**. Work
out the pieces an infantry soldier that idles, runs, fires, reloads, flinches, and dies
needs.

## How authoring works

The only sanctioned way to build the character is to **write a Blender Python (`bpy`)
script and run it through the `tcab-blend` runner** already on your `PATH`. `tcab-blend`
executes your `build.py` under headless Blender
(`blender --background --python build.py -- blender.config.json`), then exports the
skinned, animated **`character.glb`** (a standard glTF 2.0 with the skin — bones,
per-vertex weights, inverse-bind matrices — and your animations) and renders the preview
**`model.png`**. The exported `character.glb` is what is **judged**, rendered in a real
3D viewer — not the steps your script took. Anything you produce outside `tcab-blend` is
discarded.

A starter **`build.py`** is already in your workspace. It loads `blender.config.json`
(the bounding box, the output paths, and the list of required animation names), clears
the scene, and marks where to build the body mesh, the armature, the skin weights, and
each animation, then calls the bundled export helper. Fill it in with the full `bpy`
API: build the mesh (`bpy.data`/`bmesh` or primitives you edit), add an `Armature`
object with edit-bones in a hierarchy, bind the skin with vertex groups (Blender's
automatic weights are a fine starting point), and author one **Action** per animation
with F-curve keyframes on the pose bones. Carry color with vertex colors or materials so
it survives the glTF export.

Run **`tcab-blend`** whenever you want to build and see your progress — read `model.png`
after each run — and **you must run it before you finish** so `character.glb` is emitted
(an un-exported model is scored as empty). Run `tcab-blend --help` for its exact usage;
the Blender Python API is the authoring surface.

Two conventions matter:

- **Gear is baked in; the weapon is not.** The Rifleman's permanent gear (helmet, light
  armor, pouches) is part of the one skinned mesh and moves with the body. The **rifle is
  not** part of the mesh: add an empty **`weapon_socket`** bone parented to the right hand
  (with no vertex influence) where a game hangs a separate weapon asset, and shape the
  hands to hold a rifle there. Do not model the gun.
- **Author every animation in place.** The run cycle strides on the spot; a game supplies
  the real travel. Do not move the character across the world in the animations.

